capability hash/crc32  the CRC-32 of bytes or of text by the IEEE polynomial
  heroes  hash.crc32, hash.crc32_text
  go      crc32.ChecksumIEEE, crc32.IEEE, crc32.IEEETable
  rust    -
  python  zlib.crc32, binascii.crc32
  note    Go and Python answered alike for every input of the tests, a megabyte included; Go's `IEEE` polynomial and table are the `crc32` case of `Algorithm32`

capability hash/crc32c  the CRC-32C of bytes or of text, by Castagnoli's polynomial
  heroes  hash.crc32c, hash.crc32c_text
  go      crc32.Castagnoli
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go reaches it as `crc32.Checksum(data, crc32.MakeTable(crc32.Castagnoli))`

capability hash/crc32-polynomial  a CRC-32 by a polynomial of the caller's
  heroes  hash.start32, hash.KOOPMAN
  go      crc32.MakeTable, crc32.Table, crc32.Checksum, crc32.Koopman
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the polynomial is the `crc32_poly` case's `poly`, its bits reversed as Go's `MakeTable` takes it; its table is made once, by `start32` or `resume32`, and held in the state

capability hash/crc32-combine  the CRC-32 of two pieces from the CRC-32 of each and the second's length
  heroes  hash.crc32_combine
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  zlib.crc32_combine
  note    Python raises on a negative length; the length is a `u64` here, so none can be given

capability hash/adler32  the Adler-32 of bytes or of text
  heroes  hash.adler32, hash.adler32_text
  go      adler32.Checksum
  rust    -
  python  zlib.adler32
  note    Go and Python answered alike; the modulo is deferred over runs of a megabyte, and three megabytes answered as Python did

capability hash/adler32-combine  the Adler-32 of two pieces from the Adler-32 of each and the second's length
  heroes  hash.adler32_combine
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  zlib.adler32_combine

capability hash/fnv-32  FNV-1 and FNV-1a in 32 bits, over bytes or text
  heroes  hash.fnv1_32, hash.fnv1_32_text, hash.fnv1a_32, hash.fnv1a_32_text
  go      fnv.New32, fnv.New32a
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go offers them as states only; the one-shot functions and `start32` with `fnv1_32` or `fnv1a_32` are both here, the state being what Go's are

capability hash/fnv-64  FNV-1 and FNV-1a in 64 bits, over bytes or text
  heroes  hash.fnv1_64, hash.fnv1_64_text, hash.fnv1a_64, hash.fnv1a_64_text
  go      fnv.New64, fnv.New64a
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the product by the prime is written as 2^40 + 0x1b3 in halves of 32 bits, since an overflow aborts in Heroes

capability hash/fnv-128  FNV-1 and FNV-1a in 128 bits
  heroes  pending
  go      fnv.New128, fnv.New128a
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Heroes has no 128-bit number to answer in; it would answer 16 bytes, high first, as Go's `Sum` writes them

capability hash/crc64  the CRC-64 of bytes or of text by ISO's or ECMA-182's polynomial
  heroes  hash.crc64_iso, hash.crc64_iso_text, hash.crc64_ecma, hash.crc64_ecma_text
  go      crc64.ISO, crc64.ECMA, crc64.Checksum
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the register starts and ends with every bit flipped, as Go's does, so ECMA's is the `xz` format's check

capability hash/crc64-polynomial  a CRC-64 by a polynomial of the caller's
  heroes  hash.start64
  go      crc64.MakeTable, crc64.Table
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the polynomial is the `crc64_poly` case's `poly`, its bits reversed as Go's `MakeTable` takes it

capability hash/steps  a checksum or hash fed piece by piece, its value read at any moment
  heroes  hash.start32, hash.update32, hash.update32_text, hash.start64, hash.update64, hash.update64_text
  go      crc32.New, crc32.NewIEEE, crc64.New, adler32.New, hash.Hash32.Sum32, hash.Hash64.Sum64
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the state is a record the caller holds, `State32` or `State64`, whose `value` is the answer; Go's `Reset` is a new `start32` or `start64`, and Go's `Write` is `update32`, which cannot fail

capability hash/resume  a checksum or hash taken up again from a value saved before
  heroes  hash.resume32, hash.resume64
  go      crc32.Update, crc64.Update
  rust    -
  python  zlib.crc32, zlib.adler32, binascii.crc32
  note    Python takes the value as a second argument; it reduces an Adler-32 whose halves are past 65521 before it starts, 917518 from 0xffffffff and no bytes, and so does this

capability hash/copy  a copy of a state, to go on from one prefix in two ways
  heroes  built-in =
  go      hash.Cloner
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    a state is a value, so `b = a` copies it and nothing can fail

capability hash/crc16  CRC-16 by the CCITT polynomial, from a value of the caller's
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  binascii.crc_hqx

capability hash/xxh32  xxHash's XXH32 of bytes or of text, with a seed
  heroes  hash.xxh32, hash.xxh32_text
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-19 (repairs 6), so that `compress/lz4` holds no copy of its own; none of the three standard libraries has xxHash, so it is held to xxHash's own sanity vectors (`cli/xsum_sanity_check.c`, read 2026-11-19: its buffer generator and every XXH32 row) and to Python's third-party `xxhash` 4.1.0 over the C library 0.8.4, installed in the scratch folder, for lengths about every stripe, the seeds 0 and 2^32 - 1 and text of more than one byte

capability hash/xxh64  xxHash's XXH64 of bytes or of text, with a seed
  heroes  hash.xxh64, hash.xxh64_text
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-19 (repairs 6), so that `compress/zstd` holds no copy of its own (a frame's checksum is the XXH64's low 32 bits); held as XXH32 is, every XXH64 row of xxHash's sanity check and Python's `xxhash` for the seeds 0 and 2^64 - 1; XXH3 and XXH128, which Python's `xxhash` also offers, are in none of the three standard libraries and no format Rebel reads checks with them

capability hash/xxh-steps  an XXH32 or an XXH64 fed piece by piece, its value read at any moment
  heroes  hash.Xxh32, hash.start_xxh32, hash.update_xxh32, hash.update_xxh32_text, hash.Xxh64, hash.start_xxh64, hash.update_xxh64, hash.update_xxh64_text
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-19 (repairs 6); a record apart from `State32` and `State64`, since xxHash goes on from its four lanes and the bytes held after its last stripe, which its value does not hold: copied with `=`, never taken up again from a value, as Python's `xxhash.xxh32` object is copied and has no resume; a text is fed 4096 bytes at a time, so no copy of a whole text is made

capability hash/seeded  a hash seeded afresh by each process, for the buckets of a table of the program's own
  heroes  refused: the language's `{K: V}` holds a table and hashes its own keys, Heroes has no trait a key would implement, and a fresh seed needs `os`'s random bytes, which a package of layer 0 may not use
  go      maphash.Bytes, maphash.String, maphash.Comparable, maphash.WriteComparable, maphash.Hash, maphash.Hash.BlockSize, maphash.Hash.Clone, maphash.Hash.Reset, maphash.Hash.Seed, maphash.Hash.SetSeed, maphash.Hash.Size, maphash.Hash.Sum, maphash.Hash.Sum64, maphash.Hash.Write, maphash.Hash.WriteByte, maphash.Hash.WriteString, maphash.Seed, maphash.MakeSeed, maphash.Hasher, maphash.ComparableHasher, maphash.ComparableHasher.Equal, maphash.ComparableHasher.Hash
  rust    hash::Hash, hash::Hasher, hash::BuildHasher, hash::BuildHasherDefault, hash::DefaultHasher, hash::RandomState, hash::SipHasher, hash::Hash derive
  python  -

dropped interfaces  Heroes has no interface: each state is a record of its own, and a function names the one it takes
  go      hash.Hash, hash.Hash32, hash.Hash64, hash.XOF
  rust    -
  python  -

dropped sizes  a value's size is its type's, `u32` or `u64`, and none of these has a block size a caller acts on
  go      crc32.Size, crc64.Size, adler32.Size, hash.Hash.Size, hash.Hash.BlockSize
  rust    -
  python  -

dropped sum-bytes  a value is a number, and writing a number as bytes in an order is not hashing; Go's `Sum` appends the value high byte first
  go      hash.Hash.Sum
  rust    -
  python  -
